  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)={x}^{3}-64?$\n\nI. $x=-4$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(x+4)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(x+4)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(x+4).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =0 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =(-4)^{3}-64 \\\\  & =-64-64 \\\\  & =-128 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-4$ is not a root of $f(x),$ then $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $(x+4)$ is not a factor of $f(x),$ then it does not divide $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"",
